The Institute for Medical Engineering and Science (IMES) http://imes.mit.edu/ at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), in Cambridge, Massachusetts, invites applicants to apply for tenure-track faculty positions in all areas of science and engineering with application to health sciences to begin July 1, 2027 or on a mutually agreed date thereafter. Established in 2012, IMES is an inclusive community of students, administrative staff, research staff, and faculty who research, work, educate, and learn, at the convergence of engineering, science, and medicine to transform human health for all.
Appointment will be at the assistant professor level. In special cases, however, an untenured associate or senior faculty appointment may be possible, commensurate with experience. The appointment will be made in partnership with a relevant science or engineering department. The position will have equal academic responsibilities in IMES and the partner department. Applicants must hold an MD or a PhD in a science or engineering field related to health sciences by the beginning of employment. While all research areas are of interest, IMES encourages applications from candidates whose research and teaching interests would be enhanced and accelerated through deep engagement with both basic and clinical partners, and whose research program intimately involves the development and/or application of advanced computational methods. Examples include, but are not limited to:
Clinical data generation, analysis, and sharing to improve health care delivery;
The development, testing, and analysis of devices, sensors, & interfaces (mechanical, optical, electrical, chemical), and their products, to advance health care and its delivery;
Methods and modeling to understand the impact of environmental shifts on human health;
Deriving insights into human diseases through epidemiological research and mechanistic modeling;
Improving our ability to understand, predict, and manipulate human immune responses (e.g., in the context of infectious diseases, neuro-immune interactions, or women’s health)
The successful candidate will be expected to fulfill all teaching, research, and service obligations of a tenure-track faculty member at MIT. Faculty duties include teaching courses in both the candidate’s home science/engineering department and in Health Science & Technology at the undergraduate and graduate levels, advising students, conducting original scholarly research, developing course materials at the graduate and undergraduate levels, and service to MIT and the profession.
Interested candidates should submit application materials electronically at https://facultysearches.mit.edu/imes-search/. Each application must include: a curriculum vitae; the names and addresses of three or more references; a strategic statement of research interests; a statement of teaching interests; and three representative recent publications. It is the responsibility of the candidate to arrange for reference letters to be uploaded at https://faculty-searches.mit.edu/imes-search/letters/.
Please address questions to imes-search@mit.edu. Complete application materials received by December 1, 2026 will be given priority.
MIT is an equal employment opportunity employer. We value diversity and strongly encourage applications from individuals from all identities and backgrounds. All qualified applicants will receive equitable consideration for employment based on their experience and qualifications and will not be discriminated against on the basis of race, color, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, pregnancy, religion, disability, age, genetic information, veteran status, ancestry, or national or ethnic origin. See MIT’s full policy on Nondiscrimination (https://policies.mit.edu/policies-procedures/90-relations-andresponsibilities- within-mit-community/92-nondiscrimination). Know your rights: see https://www.eeoc.gov/sites/default/files/2022- 10/EEOC_KnowYourRights_screen_reader_10_20.pdf.
Employment is contingent upon the completion of a satisfactory background check, including possible verification of any finding of misconduct (or pending investigation) from prior employers.
The pay range for a 9-month academic appointment at the entry-level Assistant Professor rank (excluding summer salary): $143,000 – $147,000. The pay offered to a selected candidate during hiring will be based on factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience and education/training, internal peer equity, and applicable legal requirements. This pay range represents base pay only and does not include any other benefits or compensation.

The BIOLOGY DEPARTMENT OF EMORY UNIVERSITY in Atlanta, GA (https://biology.emory.edu/) is a broadly based biology department within Emory’s College of Arts and Sciences. We seek candidates for a tenure-track position at any rank in the area of ecology. The successful candidate will use empirical approaches to test fundamental ecological theory. We are particularly interested in complementing existing strengths through hiring of a faculty member whose research focuses on population, community, evolutionary, or behavioral ecology, and who can teach both lecture- and field-based ecology courses. Successful applicants will have a PhD, post-doctoral research experience, a record of productivity, and a commitment to evidence-based undergraduate teaching and mentoring. Competitive nine-month salary will be determined based on the quality of application materials and the current salary distribution within the biology department.
The Department of Biology is highly collegial and collaborative, and Emory's campus offers a collaborative and well-resourced research environment in a compact footprint. The Emory population biology, ecology and evolution community includes labs across Emory College, the School of Medicine, and the Rollins School of Public Health. The greater Atlanta ecology community – including Georgia Tech, Georgia State University, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, and other institutions – provides further research opportunities. Finally, the metro Atlanta area provides opportunities for science outreach and education beyond the university.
The successful applicant will train and mentor PhD students through the Population Biology, Ecology & Evolution program (https://biomed.emory.edu/PROGRAM_SITES/PBEE/). The applicant will also contribute to the department’s undergraduate teaching mission. Emory University provides many research opportunities for our outstanding undergraduate students, and the candidate will contribute to the undergraduate research mission of the college and department. You can learn more about the history and values of the Emory Biology Department here: https://biology.emory.edu/about/abc/index.html. Emory is an equal opportunity employer, and qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status or other characteristics protected by state or federal law.
Applicants should submit:
1) A cover letter summarizing the candidate’s goals for research, teaching, and mentorship;
2) A CV;
3) A description of prior research and plan for developing an independent, externally funded research program;
4) A statement of experience and interests in teaching, including how the candidate will contribute to the Biology department’s undergraduate teaching program;
5) A statement of experience and interests in mentorship, service, and/or community outreach.
Items 3-5 should be combined and not exceed 5 pages in total. Applicants should arrange to have three confidential letters of recommendation submitted to Interfolio on their behalf. All files or inquiries should be submitted electronically at:
https://apply.interfolio.com/191783
Applications received by November 1, 2026 will be given full consideration.

The Department of Anthropology, College of Social Sciences, University of Hawai’i at Mānoa (Honolulu, Hawai’i) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in sociocultural anthropology at the rank of Assistant Professor. We seek a sociocultural anthropologist with a specialty in Indigenous health to contribute to our programs in Medical Anthropology and Applied Cultural Anthropology of Hawai’i, the Pacific, and Asia. This is a 9-month, full-time, permanent, tenure-track position, subject to position clearance and availability of funds.
More information about the Department of Anthropology is available at https://anthroplogy.manoa.hawaii.edu/ The University of Hawai’i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/
Description:
Title: Assistant Professor (Sociocultural Anthropology – Indigenous Health)
Position Number: 0082638
Hiring Unit: UHM College of Social Sciences – Department of Anthropology
Location: Manoa Campus
Date Posted: September 8, 2026
Closing Date: Continuous; review of applications to begin October 23, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received by October 22, 2026 will be given priority.
Salary Information: Commensurate with qualifications and experience
Monthly Type: 9 Month
Tenure Track: Tenure
Full Time/Part Time: Full Time
Temporary/Permanent: Permanent
Duties and Responsibilities:
1. Contribute to the department’s growing specialization in Medical Anthropology and Indigenous Health, including teaching, research, and mentoring in these areas, grounded in sustained community relationships;
2. Teach introductory and upper-division undergraduate courses as well as graduate-level seminars in sociocultural anthropology and related areas, which may include Culture and Health, Ethnographic Field Techniques, Indigenous Anthropology, Food, Health, and Society, Biomedicine and Culture, Applied Cultural Anthropology, Methodology in Cultural Anthropology, and a proposed new course in Indigenous Health;
3. Develop an active research program with a strategic vision that includes fieldwork among the communities of Hawai’i, the Pacific, and/or Asia, building on existing community relationships and trust;
4. Contribute expertise and advising to students pursuing Indigenous-focused research and careers, including undergraduate capstone projects and graduate theses;
5. Support the department’s Combined Bachelor’s and Master’s (BAM) pathway and the Applied Cultural Anthropology MA program;
6. Contribute to cross-disciplinary collaboration with campus units such as the Department of Public Health Science, the Department of Native Hawaiian Health, the John A. Burns School of Medicine (JABSOM), and the Hawai’inuiakea School of Hawaiian Knowledge;
7. Mentor undergraduate, MA, and PhD students;
8. Provide service to the department, college, university, and relevant professional communities;
9. Other duties as assigned by the Department Chair.
Minimum Qualifications:
1. PhD in Anthropology or closely related field from a university of recognized standing (ABDs will be considered; PhD must be conferred by the date of hire); 2. Demonstrated specialization in Indigenous health within sociocultural anthropology;
3. Demonstrated ability to conduct research and teach undergraduate and graduate level courses in sociocultural/medical anthropology;
4. Demonstrated deep, longstanding, and ongoing relationship with an Indigenous community or field site, reflecting sustained community-embedded engagement rather than solely externally-based research;
5. Record of scholarly and instructional achievement or promise in the field as evidenced by teaching, publications, and research;
6. Demonstrated ability to maintain professionalism in meeting and conferring with others.
Desirable Qualifications:
1. Demonstrated expertise in Hawai’i, the Pacific, and/or Asia.
2. Research and teaching experience relevant to Indigenous health, health sovereignty, food and nutrition, environmental health, multispecies ethnography, health inequalities, or community-based research.
3. Community-based research approaches developed through sustained collaboration with, and accountability to, the Indigenous community/communities in which the candidate works.
4. Demonstrated engagement with important contemporary health issues, including policy-relevant topics such as health disparities in Indigenous and Pacific Islander communities.
5. Demonstrated ability to develop comparative projects that situate Hawai’i, the Pacific, and/or Asia in a broader global health context.
6. Demonstrated ability to create opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. 7. Demonstrated potential to successfully compete for extramural and intramural research and training grants (e.g., NIH, NSF, etc.);
To Apply:
Click on the “Apply” button on the top right corner of the screen to complete an application and attach the following required documents:
1. Detailed cover letter stating how you fit the position description and satisfy the minimum and desirable qualifications, while also summarizing your Teaching, Research, and Service. Please specifically address your relationship to the Indigenous community/communities central to your research, including the nature, depth, and duration of that relationship;
2. Curriculum vitae;
3. One (1) writing sample (representative peer-reviewed journal article or dissertation chapter);
4. One (1) sample syllabus for an upper division or graduate course that you would offer here. Letters of reference will be requested for long list candidates. Finalists must be prepared to submit copies of official transcripts prior to a campus visit. Official transcripts will be required for hire. Note: If you have not applied for a position before using NeoGov, you will need to create an account.
Inquiries: Seth Quintus, Chair: 808-956-8193 / squintus@hawaii.edu
EEO, Clery Act, ADA The University of Hawai’i is an Equal Opportunity Institution and is committed to a policy of nondiscrimination in employment, including on the basis of veteran and disability status. For more information, visit: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/policies/
Employment is contingent on satisfying employment eligibility verification requirements of the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986; reference checks of previous employers; and for certain positions, criminal history record checks. In accordance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, annual campus crime statistics for the University of Hawai’i may be viewed at: https://www.hawaii.edu/titleix/help/capus-security/, or a paper copy may be obtained upon request from the respective UH Campus Security or Administrative Services Office. Accommodation Request: The University of Hawai’i complies with the provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process should contact the EEO coordinator directly. Determination on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. For further information, please refer to the following link: https://www.hawaii.edu/offices/eeo/accommodation-request/
